# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Easy
pj("V01-D-001", "Language and media", "Per-word pricing of telegrams produced a compressed style that outlived the telegram.", "Easy",
   ["When the telegraph first carried private messages, senders paid by the word, and that simple pricing rule soon reshaped the way people wrote.",
    "Articles, pronouns and polite openings were the first to go, because each one added to the cost without adding much to the meaning.",
    "Stripped of these words, the resulting style was so compressed that it acquired its own name, telegraphese, and readers learned to supply what was missing.",
    "Long after the telegram itself had faded, the habit survived in newspaper headlines and, later, in the clipped phrasing of text messages."],
   "Sentence [[1]] opens by introducing the telegraph and its per-word pricing. Sentence [[2]] names the words that were dropped to save money. Sentence [[3]] begins 'Stripped of these words', which needs the list in [[2]], and names the resulting style. Sentence [[4]] moves forward in time ('Long after the telegram itself had faded') to say the habit survived, so it closes the paragraph.",
   "Putting sentence [[3]] straight after sentence [[1]] because both speak of the style in general, which leaves 'these words' with nothing to refer to.",
   "V01D-telegram-brevity", "Easy: a clear opener, the reference 'these words' and a time marker ('Long after') fix the order.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] cannot follow sentence [[1]]: 'Stripped of these words' needs the articles, pronouns and openings listed in sentence [[2]]."),
          ("1243", "Sentence [[4]] describes what happened 'Long after the telegram itself had faded', so it cannot come before the style is even named in sentence [[3]].")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-D-002", "Science and environment", "Mesh nets let some dry coastal communities collect water from fog.", "Easy",
   ["In some coastal deserts rain almost never falls, yet thick fog drifts in from the sea on many mornings of the year.",
    "Some communities in such places capture this moisture by stretching fine mesh nets upright across the path of the fog.",
    "Tiny droplets strike the mesh, merge into larger drops and trickle down into a gutter that feeds a storage tank.",
    "Each net collects only modest amounts in this way, but a long row of them can supply enough water for drinking and small gardens."],
   "Sentence [[1]] sets the scene: no rain but frequent fog. Sentence [[2]] refers to 'such places' and 'this moisture' and introduces the nets. Sentence [[3]] explains how water gathers on 'the mesh'. Sentence [[4]] says each net collects modest amounts 'in this way', referring to the process just described, and ends with what a row of nets achieves.",
   "Placing sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]] because it also talks about the nets, though 'in this way' points back to the dripping process.",
   "V01D-fog-harvesting-nets", "Easy: 'such places', 'this moisture', 'the mesh' and 'in this way' lock each link.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[4]] says the nets collect water 'in this way', but the way is only described in sentence [[3]], so sentence [[3]] must come first.")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-D-003", "Economics and business", "Airlines overbook because some passengers predictably fail to show up.", "Easy",
   ["Airlines routinely sell more tickets for a flight than the aircraft has seats, a practice that strikes many passengers as plainly unfair.",
    "The reason is that, on almost every flight, some ticket holders fail to turn up, and an empty seat earns nothing once the doors close.",
    "By estimating how many passengers are likely to miss the flight, an airline can sell the seats those no-shows would otherwise have left empty.",
    "When the estimate proves wrong and too many people arrive, the airline must persuade some of them, usually with compensation, to take a later flight."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the practice of overbooking. Sentence [[2]] gives 'The reason': some ticket holders never turn up. Sentence [[3]] refers to 'those no-shows' and explains how the airline fills their seats by estimating. Sentence [[4]] deals with the case when 'the estimate' is wrong, so it must follow sentence [[3]].",
   "Opening with sentence [[2]] because it explains the economics, though 'The reason' needs a practice to explain.",
   "V01D-airline-overbooking", "Easy: 'The reason', 'those no-shows' and 'the estimate' each point to the sentence before.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] mentions 'those no-shows' before any passengers who fail to turn up have been introduced; that happens only in sentence [[2]]."),
          ("1243", "Sentence [[4]] speaks of 'the estimate' proving wrong, but no estimate exists until sentence [[3]].")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-D-004", "Technology", "A backup in the same room does not protect against disasters that strike the whole room.", "Easy",
   ["Many small offices protect their records by copying them every night onto a second drive kept beside the main computer.",
    "This guards well against the commonest failure, a single disk that suddenly stops working.",
    "That same arrangement, however, offers no protection against a fire, flood or theft, which would claim the original and the copy together.",
    "For that reason, careful administrators keep at least one further copy in a different building, far enough away to escape whatever strikes the first."],
   "Sentence [[1]] describes the nightly copy kept beside the computer. Sentence [[2]] ('This guards well') gives its strength. Sentence [[3]] turns with 'however' to its weakness: one disaster destroys both copies. Sentence [[4]] begins 'For that reason' and offers the remedy for that weakness, a copy in another building.",
   "Placing sentence [[3]] before sentence [[2]], which leaves 'however' without a strength to contrast with.",
   "V01D-offsite-backups", "Easy: a strength, then 'however' a weakness, then 'For that reason' a remedy.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] says 'That same arrangement, however', a contrast that needs the praise in sentence [[2]] first; and in this order 'For that reason' in sentence [[4]] would follow a disk failure, which an offsite copy is not needed for.")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-D-005", "Arts and literature", "The fair-play rule made detective stories a puzzle the reader could solve.", "Easy",
   ["Readers of classic detective stories came to expect a kind of contract between writer and audience, usually described as fair play.",
    "Under this contract, every clue the detective relies on must be shown to the reader before the solution is revealed.",
    "The rule turned each story into a puzzle that an attentive reader could, in principle, solve before the detective did.",
    "A writer who withheld the decisive clue until the last chapter therefore broke more than a rule, since doing so spoiled the puzzle readers had come to solve."],
   "Sentence [[1]] introduces the 'contract' called fair play. Sentence [[2]] ('Under this contract') states its terms. Sentence [[3]] refers to 'The rule' and says it made the story a puzzle. Sentence [[4]] draws the consequence with 'therefore': withholding a clue spoils 'the puzzle', which is only introduced in sentence [[3]].",
   "Putting sentence [[4]] straight after sentence [[2]] because both speak of clues, though 'the puzzle' in sentence [[4]] has not yet been mentioned.",
   "V01D-detective-fair-play", "Easy: 'this contract', 'The rule' and 'the puzzle' chain the sentences in one order.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[4]] says a writer who withholds a clue spoils 'the puzzle', but the story is called a puzzle only in sentence [[3]], so sentence [[3]] must come first.")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-D-006", "Language and media", "Printing corrections can increase rather than reduce a newspaper's credibility.", "Easy",
   ["Some newspapers print a daily column listing the errors they made in earlier editions, from misspelt names to wrong figures.",
    "At first sight, such a column seems to advertise carelessness and might be expected to weaken readers' trust.",
    "There is a good case, however, that it does the opposite, because readers who see mistakes openly admitted have more reason to believe the rest of what is printed.",
    "A paper that never admits an error, by contrast, gives readers no way to tell whether it makes few mistakes or simply hides them, and so earns less of that belief."],
   "Sentence [[1]] introduces the corrections column. Sentence [[2]] ('such a column') gives the first-sight view that it weakens trust. Sentence [[3]] reverses this ('the opposite') and says admitted mistakes make readers believe the rest. Sentence [[4]] contrasts a paper that never admits errors and says it earns less of 'that belief', the belief described in sentence [[3]].",
   "Placing sentence [[4]] right after sentence [[1]] because 'by contrast' seems to answer the corrections column, though 'that belief' needs sentence [[3]].",
   "V01D-newspaper-corrections", "Easy: a first-sight view, its reversal, then a contrast that refers back to 'that belief'.",
   traps=[("1423", "Sentence [[4]] ends by saying such a paper 'earns less of that belief', which refers to the belief described in sentence [[3]]; it cannot come before that sentence."),
          ("1324", "Sentence [[3]] says the column 'does the opposite', which needs the expectation in sentence [[2]] that it would weaken trust.")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-D-007", "Science and environment", "Fleshy fruit pays animals to carry a plant's seeds away.", "Easy",
   ["A plant cannot walk, so it must find other ways of carrying its seeds away from the shade and competition of the parent.",
    "One of the most successful is to wrap the seeds in sweet, brightly coloured flesh that animals are eager to eat.",
    "A bird that swallows such a fruit may fly a long distance before the seeds pass through its gut and are dropped.",
    "Many seeds are protected by hard coats that survive this passage through the gut, and some even germinate better for having made it.",
    "Seen this way, the sweet flesh is less a gift to the animal than a payment to a courier whose cargo arrives intact."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the problem: a plant must move its seeds. Sentence [[2]] gives 'One of the most successful' ways, sweet fruit. Sentence [[3]] follows 'such a fruit' into a bird's gut. Sentence [[4]] explains that seeds survive 'this passage through the gut'. Sentence [[5]] concludes ('Seen this way') that the flesh is a payment to a courier 'whose cargo arrives intact', which relies on the survival described in sentence [[4]].",
   "Ending with sentence [[4]] after the courier image, though the image's 'cargo arrives intact' depends on the survival that sentence [[4]] reports.",
   "V01D-fruit-seed-dispersal", "Easy: a problem, a solution, its mechanism and a summarising image, each tied by reference words.",
   traps=[("12354", "Sentence [[5]] says the cargo 'arrives intact', which is only established once sentence [[4]] says the seeds survive the gut; and 'this passage through the gut' in sentence [[4]] would be stranded after the summary.")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-D-008", "History and politics", "Seasonal monsoon winds set the rhythm of Indian Ocean trade.", "Easy",
   ["For centuries, merchants crossing the Indian Ocean depended on a pattern of winds that reverses its direction with the seasons.",
    "For part of the year the winds blow steadily from the southwest, and for another part they blow from the northeast.",
    "A ship could therefore sail out on one monsoon and return on the next, provided it timed its departure correctly.",
    "Missing the season meant a long wait in a foreign port, sometimes for months, until the winds turned again.",
    "These enforced stays help explain why trading towns around the ocean came to host settled communities of foreign merchants."],
   "Sentence [[1]] introduces the seasonal reversal of winds. Sentence [[2]] specifies the two directions. Sentence [[3]] draws the consequence with 'therefore': out on one monsoon, back on the next, if timed correctly. Sentence [[4]] describes 'Missing the season', the failure of that timing. Sentence [[5]] refers to 'These enforced stays', the waits of sentence [[4]], and draws the wider conclusion.",
   "Putting sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]] because both describe the sailing calendar, though 'Missing the season' presupposes the timing that sentence [[3]] sets out.",
   "V01D-monsoon-trade-winds", "Easy: general to specific, then 'therefore', then the failure case and 'These enforced stays'.",
   traps=[("12435", "Sentence [[4]] describes the cost of 'Missing the season', which only makes sense after sentence [[3]] explains that departures had to be timed; and sentence [[5]]'s 'These enforced stays' must follow sentence [[4]] directly.")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

