pj("V01-D-009", "Economics and business", "Bank runs are self-fulfilling, and deposit insurance works by removing the motive to run.", "Medium",
   ["A bank keeps only a small fraction of its deposits as cash, lending the rest to borrowers who will repay over many years.",
    "On an ordinary day this is harmless, because only a few depositors want their money back at any one time.",
    "If depositors come to fear that their bank may fail, however, each has a reason to withdraw early, before the cash runs out.",
    "Their rush to withdraw can then bring about the very failure they feared, even in a bank whose loans were perfectly sound.",
    "Deposit insurance breaks this cycle not by adding cash to the vault but by removing the reason to be first in the queue."],
   "Sentence [[1]] describes fractional reserves. Sentence [[2]] says 'this is harmless' on an ordinary day. Sentence [[3]] turns with 'however' to the case of fear, when each depositor wants to be early. Sentence [[4]] shows the result: 'Their rush' causes the failure feared. Sentence [[5]] names the remedy for 'this cycle' of fear and failure, which needs both sentence [[3]] and sentence [[4]] before it.",
   "Placing sentence [[5]] after sentence [[3]], missing that 'this cycle' is complete only when sentence [[4]] shows fear producing the failure.",
   "V01D-bank-runs-deposit-insurance", "Medium: the order follows the logic of a self-fulfilling panic (normal case, fear, rush, failure, remedy), not only the connectives.",
   traps=[("13245", "Sentence [[3]] needs the harmless ordinary case of sentence [[2]] before its 'however'; and 'Their rush' in sentence [[4]] must directly follow the depositors of sentence [[3]]."),
          ("12354", "Sentence [[5]] refers to 'this cycle', but the cycle closes only when sentence [[4]] shows the rush causing the failure; sentence [[4]] cannot follow the remedy.")])

pj("V01-D-010", "Science and environment", "Street trees cool cities by blocking sunlight and by evaporation.", "Medium",
   ["On a summer afternoon, a paved street can be markedly hotter than a park only a short walk away.",
    "Much of the difference comes from what each surface does with sunlight, since asphalt absorbs it and releases it as heat well into the night.",
    "Trees intercept that sunlight before it reaches the ground, and they also cool the air around them as water evaporates from their leaves.",
    "Planting trees along streets is therefore among the cheaper ways for a city to make its hottest neighbourhoods more bearable."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the temperature gap between street and park. Sentence [[2]] explains 'the difference' through what asphalt does with sunlight. Sentence [[3]] shows trees intercepting 'that sunlight' and cooling by evaporation. Sentence [[4]] concludes with 'therefore' that planting street trees is a cheap remedy.",
   "Starting with sentence [[2]] because it explains the science, though 'the difference' needs the comparison of sentence [[1]].",
   "V01D-street-trees-cooling", "Medium: observation, explanation, mechanism and recommendation; the solver must see that the trees sentence answers the cause named in the asphalt sentence.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] says trees intercept 'that sunlight', but sunlight is introduced only in sentence [[2]]; placed after sentence [[1]] the reference has no anchor."),
          ("2134", "Sentence [[2]] opens with 'the difference', which needs the comparison between street and park in sentence [[1]].")])

pj("V01-D-011", "Science and environment", "Beaver dams, once seen as a nuisance, are now valued for creating wetlands.", "Medium",
   ["Beavers were long regarded as pests by farmers whose fields flooded whenever the animals dammed a stream.",
    "Ecologists have since come to see the same dams as one of the most powerful ways in which a single species can reshape a landscape.",
    "The reshaping begins with the pond behind the dam, which slows the flow of water, lets sediment settle and raises the water table nearby.",
    "Around the pond a wetland forms, sheltering fish, insects and birds that the fast-flowing stream could never have supported.",
    "Some land managers now reintroduce beavers deliberately, accepting a few flooded fields as the price of a wetter and richer valley."],
   "Sentence [[1]] gives the old view of beavers as pests. Sentence [[2]] reverses it ('since', 'the same dams') with the ecologists' view that dams reshape landscapes. Sentence [[3]] begins 'The reshaping', taking up that claim, and introduces the pond. Sentence [[4]] describes the wetland 'Around the pond'. Sentence [[5]] concludes with the present-day practice, which weighs the flooded fields of sentence [[1]] against the benefits of sentences [[3]] and [[4]].",
   "Moving sentence [[2]] after the description of the pond and wetland, though 'The reshaping' in sentence [[3]] takes up the claim made in sentence [[2]].",
   "V01D-beaver-dams-wetlands", "Medium: old view, revised view, mechanism, result, practice; the mandatory pair is the claim about reshaping followed by its first stage.",
   traps=[("13425", "Sentence [[3]] begins 'The reshaping begins', which takes up the claim in sentence [[2]] that dams reshape a landscape; without sentence [[2]] before it there is no reshaping to begin."),
          ("12435", "Sentence [[4]] describes the wetland 'Around the pond', but the pond appears only in sentence [[3]].")])

pj("V01-D-012", "Economics and business", "A loss leader loses money on one item to profit from the whole basket.", "Medium",
   ["A supermarket will sometimes sell a staple such as milk or bread for less than it paid the supplier.",
    "Taken by itself, every sale of that item loses the store money, which looks like a straightforward mistake.",
    "The calculation changes once one notices that few shoppers walk out carrying only the cheap item.",
    "The loss on the staple is recovered, and more, from the full baskets of other goods bought on the same trip."],
   "Sentence [[1]] describes the below-cost price. Sentence [[2]] judges it 'Taken by itself' and finds a loss. Sentence [[3]] says 'The calculation changes' once one notices what else shoppers buy. Sentence [[4]] completes the revised calculation: the loss is recovered from the full baskets. The order follows the argument from apparent mistake to the observation that overturns it and then its consequence.",
   "Putting sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]], which states the conclusion before the observation that leads to it and leaves 'The calculation changes' with nothing left to change.",
   "V01D-loss-leader-pricing", "Medium: the order depends on the logic of the argument (apparent loss, new observation, recovered loss) rather than on strong reference words.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[4]] is the outcome of the revised calculation; placed before sentence [[3]], it announces the recovery before the observation about shoppers' baskets that explains it, and sentence [[3]] then comes too late to change anything."),
          ("1324", "Sentence [[3]] says 'The calculation changes', but the original calculation, a loss on every sale, is stated only in sentence [[2]].")])

pj("V01-D-013", "Economics and business", "Money removes barter's need for a double coincidence of wants.", "Medium",
   ["Imagine a village without money, where a shoemaker who wants bread must find a baker who happens to need shoes.",
    "Such a match is rare, and the shoemaker may spend days searching while his shoes, and his hunger, wait.",
    "A good that everyone will accept, even if they have no use for it themselves, removes the need for the match altogether.",
    "With it, the shoemaker can sell to anyone who needs shoes and buy from any baker, which is the textbook account of what money is for."],
   "Sentence [[1]] sets up the imagined village and the need for a match. Sentence [[2]] ('Such a match is rare') states the cost of that need. Sentence [[3]] introduces a good everyone accepts, which 'removes the need for the match'. Sentence [[4]] begins 'With it', referring to that good, and spells out what it allows.",
   "Placing sentence [[3]] before sentence [[2]] because both concern the match, though the solution makes sense only after the problem of rarity is stated.",
   "V01D-barter-coincidence-of-wants", "Medium: problem, its cost, the solution and its effect; the order follows the reasoning of the thought experiment.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] offers the solution before sentence [[2]] has shown the problem it solves, and 'With it' in sentence [[4]] would then refer back across the problem to the good."),
          ("1342", "Sentence [[2]] about days of searching cannot end the paragraph after the solution has already removed the need to search.")])

pj("V01-D-014", "Technology", "A safety brake, not a stronger rope, made passenger lifts and tall buildings acceptable.", "Medium",
   ["Buildings of more than five or six storeys were rare before the passenger lift, since few people would climb that far every day.",
    "Early lifts, however, hung from a single rope, and the fear that it might snap kept many people firmly on the stairs.",
    "The decisive invention was therefore not a stronger rope but a brake that gripped the guide rails automatically if the rope failed.",
    "Once passengers could trust that a broken rope meant a jolt rather than a fall, they accepted the lift as an ordinary part of daily life.",
    "Only when the lift had become that ordinary did it make commercial sense to build upward, and the tall office block followed."],
   "Sentence [[1]] explains why tall buildings needed lifts. Sentence [[2]] adds the obstacle ('however'): fear of a snapping rope. Sentence [[3]] concludes ('therefore') that the decisive invention addressed that fear, a brake. Sentence [[4]] describes the resulting trust. Sentence [[5]] ('Only when the lift had become that ordinary') states the final consequence, tall buildings. The chain runs from need to obstacle to solution to trust to consequence.",
   "Placing sentence [[5]] before sentence [[4]], though 'that ordinary' points back to the lift becoming 'an ordinary part of daily life' in sentence [[4]].",
   "V01D-elevator-safety-brakes", "Medium: a causal chain (need, fear, brake, trust, building) in which each step depends on the previous one.",
   traps=[("12354", "Sentence [[5]] says building upward made sense only once the lift 'had become that ordinary', which points back to the lift as 'an ordinary part of daily life' in sentence [[4]], so sentence [[4]] must come first."),
          ("13245", "Sentence [[3]] says the invention was 'therefore' a brake, a conclusion that follows only from the fear of a snapping rope described in sentence [[2]].")])

pj("V01-D-015", "Technology", "Refrigeration loosened diets' dependence on preserved food.", "Medium",
   ["Before household refrigeration, food that could not be eaten quickly had to be salted, smoked, dried or pickled to keep it from spoiling.",
    "Diets were shaped accordingly, leaning heavily on preserved meat and fish in winter and on whatever was fresh in its brief season.",
    "The refrigerator loosened this dependence by slowing spoilage without changing the taste or texture of the food.",
    "Fresh milk, meat and vegetables could now be kept for days, and the strong flavours of preserved food gradually lost their central place on the table."],
   "Sentence [[1]] describes preservation before refrigeration. Sentence [[2]] says diets were shaped 'accordingly'. Sentence [[3]] says the refrigerator loosened 'this dependence', the reliance on preserved food just described. Sentence [[4]] gives the result ('now'): fresh food kept for days and preserved flavours in decline.",
   "Placing sentence [[3]] straight after sentence [[1]], though 'this dependence' refers to the diet described in sentence [[2]].",
   "V01D-refrigeration-diet-change", "Medium: before and after, with 'accordingly' and 'this dependence' as the links; the solver must see the dependence is the diet.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] speaks of 'this dependence', which is the reliance on preserved food in the diet of sentence [[2]]; and 'accordingly' in sentence [[2]] makes no sense after the refrigerator has arrived.")])

pj("V01-D-016", "Arts and literature", "Jazz improvisation is fluency within a structure, not freedom from rules.", "Medium",
   ["To a newcomer, a jazz solo can sound as if the player is simply making it up, free of any rule.",
    "In fact the soloist works within a tight framework, a fixed sequence of chords that repeats beneath every chorus.",
    "Each note is chosen against that sequence, and the listener's pleasure depends partly on hearing the line bend towards it and away again.",
    "Improvisation, on this view, is not the absence of structure but a fluency within it, much as conversation is unscripted yet grammatical."],
   "Sentence [[1]] gives the newcomer's impression of rule-free playing. Sentence [[2]] corrects it ('In fact') with the framework of chords. Sentence [[3]] shows each note chosen against 'that sequence'. Sentence [[4]] states the conclusion 'on this view', that improvisation is fluency within structure.",
   "Opening with sentence [[2]] because it states the main fact, though 'In fact' corrects an impression that only sentence [[1]] supplies.",
   "V01D-jazz-improvisation", "Medium: impression, correction, illustration, conclusion; the order follows the argument against a misconception.",
   traps=[("2134", "Sentence [[2]] begins 'In fact', which corrects a mistaken impression; that impression is stated in sentence [[1]], so sentence [[1]] must open."),
          ("1243", "Sentence [[4]] draws the conclusion 'on this view' before the illustration, and sentence [[3]]'s 'that sequence' would then be separated from the chords it refers to.")])

pj("V01-D-017", "Language and media", "Radio drama gains power from the absence of pictures.", "Medium",
   ["Radio drama is often treated as a poor relation of film, a form that merely lacks pictures.",
    "Many who work in it have argued the reverse, that the absence of pictures is precisely what the form exploits.",
    "A listener told that a castle looms over a stormy sea builds that castle in imagination, and builds it to suit his or her own fears.",
    "No set designer can compete with a castle that each listener has built privately, which is why radio can still frighten people whom a screen leaves unmoved."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the common view that radio merely lacks pictures. Sentence [[2]] argues 'the reverse'. Sentence [[3]] illustrates with the listener who builds a castle in imagination. Sentence [[4]] draws the conclusion from that example: no designer can compete with 'a castle that each listener has built privately', a phrase that points back to the castle of sentence [[3]].",
   "Placing sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]] because it states the claim, though 'a castle that each listener has built privately' needs the castle example to have been given first.",
   "V01D-radio-drama-imagination", "Medium: view, counter-view, example, conclusion; the conclusion depends on the example for its privately built castle.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[4]] refers to 'a castle that each listener has built privately', which presupposes the castle introduced in sentence [[3]]; the conclusion cannot precede it."),
          ("3124", "Sentence [[3]] could seem a vivid opener, but then sentence [[2]]'s 'the reverse' would be separated from the view it reverses, and the example would come before the claim it supports.")])

pj("V01-D-018", "History and politics", "Relay stations let messages travel faster than any single rider.", "Medium",
   ["A single rider, however strong his horse, can cover only so much ground in a day before both of them need rest.",
    "Several large empires overcame this limit by building stations at intervals along their main roads, each stocked with fresh horses.",
    "A message was handed from rider to rider, or carried by one rider changing mounts, so that it never had to stop while an animal recovered.",
    "News could thus cross a vast territory in a fraction of the time any single traveller would need, which gave distant rulers a firmer hold over their provinces."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the limit on a single rider. Sentence [[2]] says empires overcame 'this limit' with relay stations. Sentence [[3]] explains how the relay worked. Sentence [[4]] concludes with 'thus' about the speed of news and its political effect.",
   "Opening with sentence [[2]] because it introduces the empires, though 'this limit' requires the single rider of sentence [[1]].",
   "V01D-postal-relay-riders", "Medium: limit, solution, mechanism, consequence; each step answers the one before.",
   traps=[("2134", "Sentence [[2]] refers to 'this limit', which is stated only in sentence [[1]]."),
          ("1243", "Sentence [[4]] says news 'thus' crossed the territory quickly; the reason, that the message never stopped, is given in sentence [[3]], which must come first.")])

pj("V01-D-019", "History and politics", "Monastic prayer hours created a need for reliable timekeeping.", "Medium",
   ["Monastic life was organised around prayers held at fixed hours of the day and night, including some long before dawn.",
    "Keeping to this timetable posed a practical problem, since sundials are useless at night and water clocks can freeze in winter.",
    "Monasteries therefore relied on bells, rung by a monk charged with judging the hour, to call the community to prayer.",
    "Some historians have suggested that the same need helped encourage the development of mechanical clocks that could strike the hours on their own."],
   "Sentence [[1]] describes the fixed prayer hours. Sentence [[2]] names the problem of keeping 'this timetable'. Sentence [[3]] gives the monasteries' answer ('therefore'), bells rung by a monk judging the hour. Sentence [[4]] links 'the same need' to mechanical clocks that strike on their own, which contrasts with the human bell-ringer of sentence [[3]].",
   "Placing sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]] because both concern timekeeping, though clocks that strike 'on their own' answer the human bell-ringer.",
   "V01D-monastery-bells-timekeeping", "Medium: need, problem, practical answer, later development; the solver must see that the clock sentence extends the bell-ringer sentence.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[4]]'s clocks that strike 'on their own' are contrasted with the monk who rings the bells by judgement in sentence [[3]]; and 'therefore' in sentence [[3]] must follow the problem in sentence [[2]] directly.")])

pj("V01-D-020", "Arts and literature", "Serial publication made Victorian novels suspenseful and open to readers' influence.", "Medium",
   ["Many Victorian novels first reached readers not as complete books but in monthly or weekly instalments.",
    "A writer working this way had to end each part with a reason to buy the next, which encouraged suspense at regular intervals.",
    "Serial publication had a second consequence: because instalments appeared while the story was still being written, readers' reactions could reach the author before the ending was settled.",
    "The serial was thus less a finished work released in pieces than a story shaped, in part, by its audience as it went along."],
   "Sentence [[1]] introduces publication in instalments. Sentence [[2]] gives the first effect, suspense, for a writer 'working this way'. Sentence [[3]] announces 'a second consequence', readers' reactions reaching the author. Sentence [[4]] concludes ('thus') that the serial was 'shaped, in part, by its audience', which rests on sentence [[3]].",
   "Ending with sentence [[3]], or placing it before sentence [[2]], without noticing that 'a second consequence' needs a first one and that the conclusion about the audience rests on it.",
   "V01D-serialised-novels", "Medium: two consequences in order and a conclusion drawn from the second; the logic of what supports the claim that the story was shaped by its audience decides the end.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] speaks of 'a second consequence', which requires the first consequence, suspense, in sentence [[2]] before it."),
          ("1243", "Sentence [[4]] concludes the story was shaped by its audience, but that depends on readers' reactions reaching the author, which sentence [[3]] supplies.")])

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Difficult
pj("V01-D-021", "Economics and business", "A long warranty signals quality because a poor product could not afford to offer it.", "Difficult",
   ["We tend to think of a long warranty as insurance, for which the buyer pays a little more and is protected if the product fails.",
    "Yet the warranty may matter most to buyers who never make a claim, because of what its mere existence reveals.",
    "A firm selling a fragile product could not offer years of free repairs without being ruined by the claims that would follow.",
    "A firm confident that its product will last, by contrast, expects few claims and can make the same promise cheaply.",
    "The offer thus carries information about quality that no advertisement could, precisely because a poor product could not cheaply imitate it."],
   "Sentence [[1]] gives the familiar view of a warranty as insurance. Sentence [[2]] turns ('Yet') to what a warranty reveals even when no claim is made. Sentences [[3]] and [[4]] supply the reasoning: a fragile product's maker could not afford the promise, while a confident maker, 'by contrast', can make 'the same promise' cheaply. Sentence [[5]] concludes ('thus') that the offer carries information because a poor product could not imitate it, which is exactly the contrast just drawn.",
   "Treating the two firms as interchangeable examples and reversing them, or reading the paragraph as being about insurance and ending with sentence [[2]].",
   "V01D-warranties-signal-quality", "Difficult: a signalling argument; the solver must see that the 'Yet' sentence states the thesis, that the confident firm is defined against the fragile one, and that the conclusion depends on that contrast.",
   traps=[("12435", "Sentence [[4]] describes the confident firm 'by contrast' and makes 'the same promise'; both need the fragile firm and its promise of years of free repairs in sentence [[3]] first."),
          ("13452", "Sentence [[2]]'s 'Yet' turns away from the insurance view of sentence [[1]]; placed last it would contrast with the conclusion it is supposed to introduce, and sentence [[3]] would follow sentence [[1]] with no reason given for discussing what firms can afford.")],
   exams=HARD)

pj("V01-D-022", "Arts and literature", "Photography did not just push painters away from appearances; it also taught them new ways of seeing.", "Difficult",
   ["A familiar story holds that photography freed painting from the duty of recording how things look.",
    "Once a machine could capture a face or a street more exactly than any hand, the argument runs, painters turned to what the camera could not show.",
    "The account has some truth, but its picture of painters retreating to ground the camera could not reach makes them sound like workers displaced by a machine.",
    "Far from feeling displaced, many of them seem to have been fascinated by photographs, borrowing their cropped edges, frozen movement and unusual angles.",
    "The camera, on this view, did not merely push painting away from appearances; it also taught painters new ways of seeing them."],
   "Sentence [[1]] introduces the 'familiar story'. Sentence [[2]] spells it out ('the argument runs'). Sentence [[3]] grants it 'some truth' but objects to its picture of painters 'retreating to ground the camera could not reach', which restates sentence [[2]] and so must follow it. Sentence [[4]] answers that picture ('Far from feeling displaced'): painters were fascinated by photographs. Sentence [[5]] states the revised view 'on this view', combining the old story with the correction.",
   "Moving the critique in sentence [[3]] directly after sentence [[1]], since both refer to the story, without noticing that sentence [[3]] objects to a detail first given in sentence [[2]].",
   "V01D-photography-painting-realism", "Difficult: a reported view, its elaboration, a concession with an objection, and a revised view; the tempting order puts the critique before the elaboration it criticises.",
   traps=[("13245", "Sentence [[3]] criticises the picture of painters 'retreating to ground the camera could not reach', a detail supplied only by sentence [[2]]'s 'turned to what the camera could not show'; the critique cannot precede it, and sentence [[4]]'s 'Far from feeling displaced' must answer sentence [[3]]'s picture of displaced workers."),
          ("21345", "Sentence [[2]] contains 'the argument runs', which reports a view already introduced; sentence [[1]] must name the 'familiar story' first.")],
   exams=HARD)

pj("V01-D-023", "Language and media", "Contradictory proverbs are not rival laws but tools for different situations.", "Difficult",
   ["Proverbs are often mocked for contradicting one another: we are told to look before we leap, and also that he who hesitates is lost.",
    "If proverbs were meant as general laws, the mockery would be deserved, since two laws that point in opposite directions cannot both hold.",
    "But nobody uses a proverb as a law; it is offered in a particular situation, to name a consideration the listener may be neglecting.",
    "Said to someone about to sign a contract unread, the first proverb fits; said to someone dithering over an expiring offer, the second does.",
    "Neither proverb is refuted by the situation that suits the other; contradictory proverbs are less like rival theories than like opposite tools in the same kit, each suited to a different job."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the charge with the two proverbs. Sentence [[2]] grants it conditionally ('If proverbs were meant as general laws'). Sentence [[3]] denies the condition ('But nobody uses a proverb as a law') and states how proverbs are used. Sentence [[4]] illustrates that use with two situations. Sentence [[5]] says neither proverb is refuted by 'the situation that suits the other', referring to the two situations of sentence [[4]], and concludes with the image of opposite tools in one kit.",
   "Putting the two situations of sentence [[4]] before the general principle of sentence [[3]], so that 'But' no longer contrasts with the conditional of sentence [[2]].",
   "V01D-contradictory-proverbs", "Difficult: a conditional concession and its rebuttal; the solver must see that 'But' answers the conditional 'If' sentence and that the example illustrates the rebuttal rather than replacing it.",
   traps=[("12435", "Sentence [[3]]'s 'But nobody uses a proverb as a law' denies the condition in sentence [[2]]; placed after the example in sentence [[4]], which already defends the proverbs, 'But' would have nothing to contrast with."),
          ("13245", "Sentence [[2]] grants the mockery conditionally; placed after sentence [[3]] has denied that proverbs are laws, it would reopen a case already answered.")],
   exams=HARD)

pj("V01-D-024", "Technology", "Lighthouse automation priced the keeper's job as written rather than as done.", "Difficult",
   ["Automation is usually judged by comparing what a machine costs with the wages of the person it replaces.",
    "The lighthouses that lost their keepers in the last century were judged in just this way, and on those terms the machines won easily.",
    "Yet the keepers had also logged the weather, watched for ships in distress and reported wrecks, none of which entered the comparison.",
    "The arithmetic was not so much wrong as incomplete, because it priced the job as written rather than the job as done."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the general way automation is judged. Sentence [[2]] applies it ('in just this way') to lighthouses, where machines won. Sentence [[3]] turns ('Yet') to the keepers' unrecorded work, which the comparison left out. Sentence [[4]] gives the verdict on 'The arithmetic': incomplete, because it ignored the job as done, the work listed in sentence [[3]].",
   "Placing the verdict of sentence [[4]] before the evidence of sentence [[3]], so that 'Yet' would have to contrast with a criticism rather than with the machines' easy win.",
   "V01D-lighthouse-automation", "Difficult: the last two sentences can look reversible; the solver must see that 'Yet' needs the machines' victory to oppose and that the verdict on the arithmetic presupposes the omitted work.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[3]]'s 'Yet' must oppose the claim that the machines won easily in sentence [[2]]; after sentence [[4]] has already called the arithmetic incomplete, 'Yet' would oppose nothing, and the verdict would come before its evidence."),
          ("2134", "Sentence [[2]] says the lighthouses were judged 'in just this way', which needs the general method of judging described in sentence [[1]].")],
   exams=HARD)

pj("V01-D-025", "Science and environment", "Terraces protect slopes by shortening the water's run, not by holding back rain.", "Difficult",
   ["Rain falling on a bare slope gathers speed as it runs downhill, and the faster it flows the more soil it tears loose and carries away.",
    "What matters most, then, is not how much rain falls but how long a stretch of unbroken slope the water can travel.",
    "A terrace shortens that stretch to the width of a single step, on which the water slows, spreads and sinks into the ground.",
    "Terraces therefore protect a hillside not by holding back the rain, which no earthwork could do in a heavy storm, but by denying the water its running start."],
   "Sentence [[1]] gives the mechanism: water gains speed as it runs downhill and erodes more. Sentence [[2]] draws the principle ('then'): the length of unbroken slope matters more than the amount of rain. Sentence [[3]] shows a terrace shortening 'that stretch', a term introduced in sentence [[2]]. Sentence [[4]] concludes ('therefore') that terraces work by denying the water its running start, picking up the contrast with the amount of rain in sentence [[2]].",
   "Placing sentence [[3]] straight after sentence [[1]], since both describe water on a slope, without seeing that 'that stretch' and the principle it applies come from sentence [[2]].",
   "V01D-hillside-terracing-erosion", "Difficult: the order depends on seeing that the 'then' sentence turns the mechanism into a principle about distance rather than quantity, which the terrace sentence and the conclusion then apply.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] shortens 'that stretch', but the stretch of unbroken slope is named only in sentence [[2]]; and sentence [[2]]'s 'then' draws a principle from the mechanism in sentence [[1]], not from the terrace."),
          ("1342", "Sentence [[2]] states the principle that the terrace and the conclusion apply; placed last it would follow the conclusion it supports, and 'that stretch' in sentence [[3]] would lack a referent.")],
   exams=HARD)
